From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 02:14:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(voiceprofile): the leases of a process that is gone do
 not outlive a deleted profile

A synthesis takes a request-scoped lease on a profile's reference clip: a
hard link under voice-profiles/.leases/<store>/, removed when the request
ends, so that an administrator's delete does not break a synthesis in
flight. A process that is killed removes nothing. Its lease directory
stays, and a lease in it keeps the recording's bytes on disk after the
profile is deleted - behind a link no listing of profiles shows. Seen: a
lease four weeks old, linked to a clip of a living profile, beside eight
empty directories of processes that had ended without Close.

A Store now keeps an owner file locked in its lease directory for as long
as it lives (flock on Unix, LockFileEx on Windows, as the add-on stores
do); the kernel drops the lock when the process ends, however it ends.
Lease directories whose owner file can be locked belong to nobody and are
removed, with every lease in them, when a Store starts and whenever a
profile is deleted. A living Store's leases are left alone: a synthesis in
flight keeps its audio until it completes, as before. A directory without
an owner file (left by a LocalAI that wrote none) is removed once the last
lease made or released in it is a day old - counted by the directory's own
time, since a lease is a link and carries its clip's.

Only real directories are considered: a link under .leases is not followed
and a stray file is not removed.

Tests: a killed process's lease is gone with the profile's delete, and no
WAV is left below the store; a living store's lease survives the delete
and is readable until released; a new Store removes what the ones before
it left; a directory without an owner file goes only once it is old; a
symlink and a stray file are left alone; the owner file is 0600 and gone
at Close.

Assisted-by: Claude Code
